  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-103153",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The center of the expansion is $x=4,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $5,$ i.e., $R\\ge 5.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-1,9).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-1,9).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14218229/assets/c0f5ebe0de173431758aed0680a6795267582aa2bc209702253d3d2828c91d02.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $3\\in [-1,9),$ the series is convergent at $x=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-2\\notin [-1,9),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $1\\in [-1,9),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
